AtlasCMake and AtlasLCG on macOS with a (non-Apple) Clang, e.g. conda-forge's:
1. no GNU ld options (--as-needed, --no-undefined, -z max-page-size, --hash-style): Apple's
   linker knows none of them, whichever Clang drives it (only AppleClang was excluded);
2. rpaths, when CMAKE_INSTALL_RPATH is given: System Integrity Protection drops
   DYLD_LIBRARY_PATH from the environment of every system executable (/bin/bash,
   /usr/bin/env), so libraries cannot rely on it to find each other. Nothing changes elsewhere;
3. no scanning for C++ modules by default (CMake does it from C++20 on): ATLAS code has none,
   and with Clang the scan needs clang-scan-deps;
4. the build's private bash: a link to a bash outside the system directories when there is one
   (conda-forge's), which SIP does not protect either. Current macOS versions kill the copy of
   /bin/bash that AtlasCMake makes;
5. libpython linked into executables only: a library that links it, loaded into a python
   interpreter with libpython built in (conda-forge's), runs against a second, uninitialised
   copy of it (two-level namespace). Libraries look the symbols up at run time instead;
6. setup.sh also puts the project's lib/ into GAUDI_PLUGIN_PATH and ROOT_LIBRARY_PATH on macOS,
   where Gaudi's plugin service and ROOT find libraries without DYLD_LIBRARY_PATH (point 2).
7. C++23 by default on macOS with Clang 20 and 21 too (ATLAS switches at Clang 22):
   conda-forge's ROOT is a C++23 build with Clang 20 there, and its interface would otherwise
   compile part of a project as C++23 and the rest as C++20.
8. the .components file of a library from the library's own file name: a shared library (the
   T/P converter factories) has another suffix than a module on macOS (.dylib, not .so).
9. atlas_disable_as_needed() also removes -dead_strip_dylibs, Apple's linker's counterpart of
   --as-needed (in conda-forge's LDFLAGS): packages that link a library only for what its static
   initialisers register (L1Topo's algorithms) lost it.

